※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=3204

※ What may be written before a lambda is what a caller reads off the type, and
※ that is `listable` and nothing else so far.  Everything else a function can
※ say about itself -- how it is inlined, whether it is pure, what convention it
※ follows -- is about its body, and a body is not what a name holds: a lambda
※ handed from one name to another carries two addresses and its type, and an
※ attribute the type does not carry is one the caller never hears.
※
※ So the list is checked against a target of its own, and `inline` written here
※ is an attribute that does not apply to a lambda or a function type.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let f: fn(u8) → u8 = @[inline] λ a: u8 → u8:
        a + a
    if f(1u8) ≠ 2u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
